similar fate as the  two Americans that we've seen so far is  there an obvious military strategy to  deal with Isis they seem to be very  fast-moving and to have made quick  territorial gains well there are the  ultimate aim of course has to be that  the ground forces of Iraq hand of the  cards will be able to deal with Isis but  for the moment  the ISIS forces are too strong they need  to be degraded their command and control  needs to be diminished and their lines  of communication need to be interrupted  that will require air power that they  simply don't seem to have and then  further down the line worthy to launch a  ground counter-offensive they would  require close air support and again they  wouldn't be able to provide that  themselves although it's possible that  other allies not Britain or the United  States Arab countries would be able to  do that do you think they are inclined  to do that I think that they might but I  think from our perspective we have to  recognize the huge threat that Isis  actually pose humanitarian threat in the  region destabilization of the wider  region and as a source of jihadists who  would they will export if they're given  the chance they are our enemy they have  to be defeated as far as Ukraine is  concerned you said you think that's more  of a political problem yet fighting is  going on why do you say that well I  think that the the root of the problem  is that the West has allowed Putin to  prod us without adequate responses from  the cyber attack on Estonia to the  invasion of George or the cutting off of  Ukraine's gas I think all these have  emboldened President Putin I think that  at heart they have a very different  world view they still believe that they  should have a sphere of influence and  all the old Soviet thought process that  says they should have a veto prospect on  the copies that were as a well so what  you know they used to occupy these  countries why not let our view is very  clear sovereign nations must be allowed  to exercise self determination that's  quite a big gap between our world view  and the Putin worldview and that really  does lie at the heart of his approach  but you Said's been prodding us do you  think he can be pushed back without a  much more serious conflict well I think  it's up to the west now and we have a  perfect opportunity at the NATO summit  in Wales to say we're not going to put  up with this we are going to reinforce  NATO we are going to make sure that it's  perfectly clear that article 5 that an  attack on one is an attack on all that  that is going to be fully respected and  I think we need to have a permanent  force in the Baltic States upgrade at  baltic air defenses upgrade our  relations and  preparations with Poland and to have  increased numbers of NATO exercise I  mean you mentioned Poland one of the  kind of public spokesman who tends to  back Putin Putin has and endorses but  they've talked on Russian television  about maybe new king Poland I mean  doesn't that really scare you well  there's a lot of very loose talk and  none of this of course helps to diminish  the tension but you do have President  Putin who has dramatically increased his  defense budget including on new nuclear  programs he doesn't maintain a first use  doctrine or on nuclear weapons and I  think that this is a this is telling us  increasingly about the salience of the  nuclear weapons issue finally you've  been writing about this today about NATO  members spending two percent of their  gdp on defense but truth is we're not  going to do that according to our own  redbook projections in this country we  know we really serious about defending  ourselves too well I think we should i  think that there's a great opportunity  for the prime minister to set at a  conservative position that says as the  economy grows we will continue to spend  two percent of our gdp on defense that  is what we're asking other NATO members  to do currently only four of us do that  you've got Poland coming up you've got  others coming up we should lead by  example and we should say Britain is  making very clear our commitment to our  collective security it's now up to  others to step up to the plate if we all  want the insurance policy we should all  be paying the premiums dr. Fox thank you
 

